  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-finite-dimensional-integral-jensen-for-convex-loss": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $d\\geq1$ and let $C$ be a\nnonempty closed convex subset of $\\mathbb R^d$, with closedness in the Euclidean\ntopology ([[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]]) and convexity as in\n[[def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space]]. Let $\\mu$ be a probability measure on\nits Borel sets, and assume $\\int_C\\|a\\|_2\\,\\mu(da)<\\infty$, where $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$\nis the Euclidean norm from [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Define the barycenter\n$b=\\int_Ca\\,\\mu(da)$ coordinatewise and assume $b\\in C$. If\n$f:C\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is Borel measurable and convex, then\n$$f(b)\\leq\\int_C f(a)\\,\\mu(da).$$\nHere convexity uses [[def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets]],\nand the barycenter is the vector of coordinate integrals."
    ],
    "lem-prior-and-model-kernel-define-joint-law": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ and $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ be measurable spaces,\nlet $\\pi$ be a probability measure on $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$, and let\n$K:\\Theta\\times\\mathcal A\\to[0,1]$ be a probability kernel. For\n$C\\in\\mathcal T\\otimes\\mathcal A$, write\n$C_\\theta=\\{x\\in\\mathcal X:(\\theta,x)\\in C\\}$ and set\n$$J(C)=\\int_\\Theta K(\\theta,C_\\theta)\\,\\pi(d\\theta).$$\nThen $J$ is a probability measure on the product sigma-algebra, and for every\nnonnegative product-measurable $h$,\n$$\\int_{\\Theta\\times\\mathcal X}h(\\theta,x)\\,J(d\\theta,dx)=\\int_\\Theta\\left(\\int_\\mathcal Xh(\\theta,x)\\,K(\\theta,dx)\\right)\\pi(d\\theta).$$\nIn particular, $J(A\\times B)=\\int_AK(\\theta,B)\\,\\pi(d\\theta)$ for\n$A\\in\\mathcal T$ and $B\\in\\mathcal A$."
    ],
    "def-loss-function-and-risk": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]), let\n$(\\mathsf D,\\mathcal G)$ be a measurable action space, meaning a set with\na specified sigma-algebra ([[def-measurable-space]]), and let\n$L:\\Theta\\times\\mathsf D\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be a loss function whose section\n$a\\mapsto L(\\theta,a)$ is $\\mathcal G$-measurable for each $\\theta$. A\nrandomized decision rule is a probability kernel $\\delta$ from the data space\nto the action space ([[def-randomized-procedure-as-a-markov-kernel]]). Its\n**risk** at $\\theta$ is the extended nonnegative number\n$$R(\\theta,\\delta)=\\int_{\\mathcal X}\\left(\\int_{\\mathsf D}L(\\theta,a)\\,\\delta(x,da)\\right)P_\\theta(dx).$$\nThe inner integral is a measurable function of $x$, by\n[[lem-procedure-action-law-is-measurable]], so the outer nonnegative integral\nis defined by [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]].\nA deterministic measurable rule is included by taking its point-mass action\nkernel. No measurability on $\\Theta$ is required for this definition."
    ],
    "def-risk-domination-and-admissibility": [
      "## Definition\n\nFor two decision rules $\\delta_0,\\delta_1$ in one decision problem,\n$\\delta_1$ **dominates** $\\delta_0$ if\n$R(\\theta,\\delta_1)\\leq R(\\theta,\\delta_0)$ for every parameter $\\theta$,\nwith strict inequality for at least one $\\theta$. A rule is **admissible** if\nno decision rule dominates it; otherwise it is inadmissible. Risk is the\nextended-valued function of [[def-loss-function-and-risk]]. Admissibility is\na pointwise comparison across the whole parameter set; it does not assert that\nthe rule minimizes risk at any particular parameter or under a global\ncriterion."
    ],
    "def-bayes-and-minimax-risk": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $\\Theta$ be a nonempty measurable parameter space, let the model be a\nprobability kernel from $\\Theta$ to the data space, and let\n$(\\mathsf D,\\mathcal G)$ be a nonempty measurable action space\n([[def-measurable-space]]). Let $L:\\Theta\\times\\mathsf D\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$\nbe measurable from the product measurable space with sigma-algebra\n$\\mathcal T\\otimes\\mathcal G$ to the extended real line with its Borel\nsigma-algebra, and assume $L(\\theta,a)\\geq0$ for all $(\\theta,a)$\n([[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]],\n[[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]], [[def-extended-reals]],\n[[def-borel-sigma-algebra-on-the-extended-real-line]]). A **prior** $\\pi$ is a\nprobability measure on $\\Theta$. The risk function $\\theta\\mapsto R(\\theta,\\delta)$ is measurable\nunder these hypotheses by kernel integration\n([[def-parameter-measurable-statistical-model-as-markov-kernel]],\n[[thm-measurability-of-integration-against-a-kernel]]). The **Bayes risk** of\n$\\delta$ under $\\pi$ is\n$$r_\\pi(\\delta)=\\int_\\Theta R(\\theta,\\delta)\\,\\pi(d\\theta)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nIts **worst-case risk** is $R^*(\\delta)=\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}\nR(\\theta,\\delta)$, and the **minimax value** is\n$$V_{\\rm mm}=\\inf\\{R^*(\\delta):\\delta\\text{ is a measurable randomized rule from data to actions}\\}.$$\nA rule is minimax if its worst-case risk equals this infimum. These definitions\nmake no assertion that a minimizer exists. Nonemptiness of $\\mathsf D$ ensures\nthe rule class is nonempty: fixing any $a_0\\in\\mathsf D$ gives the constant\npoint-mass rule $x\\mapsto\\delta_{a_0}$."
    ],
    "thm-randomization-does-not-improve-convex-action-loss": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $d\\geq1$ and let the\naction space be a nonempty closed convex set $\\mathsf D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$,\nwith its Borel sigma-algebra ([[def-measurable-space]]). Closedness is in the\nEuclidean topology ([[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]]) and convexity\nuses [[def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space]]. Let\n$\\delta(x,da)$ be a randomized decision rule and let $L(\\theta,\\cdot)$ be a\nnonnegative Borel convex loss on $\\mathsf D$\n([[def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets]]). Assume for\nevery $\\theta$ that $\\int_\\mathsf D\\|a\\|_2\\,\\delta(x,da)<\\infty$ and the\nbarycenter $b_\\delta(x)=\\int_\\mathsf D a\\,\\delta(x,da)$ belongs to $\\mathsf D$\nfor $P_\\theta$-almost every $x$. Then there is a measurable deterministic\nrule $d$ such that\n$$R(\\theta,d)\\leq R(\\theta,\\delta)\\qquad(\\theta\\in\\Theta).$$\nThe barycenter is taken coordinatewise; finite first moment in the Euclidean\nnorm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] makes each\ncoordinate integral finite. No claim is made for nonconvex action sets or when\nthe barycenter condition fails."
    ],
    "thm-bayes-rule-minimizes-prior-risk": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a probability\nkernel from a standard-Borel parameter space $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ to a\nstandard-Borel data space $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$, where standard Borel means\nmeasurably isomorphic to a Polish space\n([[def-standard-borel-space]]). Let $\\pi$ be a prior,\nand let $L:\\Theta\\times\\mathsf D\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be jointly measurable for a\nmeasurable action space $(\\mathsf D,\\mathcal G)$. Let $J$ be the joint prior-data\nlaw of [[lem-prior-and-model-kernel-define-joint-law]], let $M$ be its data\nmarginal, and let $\\Pi_x(d\\theta)$ be a regular posterior distribution of\n$\\theta$ given $x$ (the disintegration is supplied by\n[[thm-disintegration-of-a-joint-law-on-standard-borel-spaces]]).\nDefine posterior risk\n$$q(x,a)=\\int_\\Theta L(\\theta,a)\\,\\Pi_x(d\\theta).$$\nSuppose a measurable deterministic rule $d^*:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathsf D$\nattains the posterior minimum for $M$-almost every $x$; that is,\n$q(x,d^*(x))\\leq q(x,a)$ for every $a\\in\\mathsf D$ on one measurable\n$M$-full-measure set. Then $d^*$ minimizes prior-integrated risk among all randomized decision\nrules $\\delta$:\n$$r_\\pi(d^*)\\leq r_\\pi(\\delta).$$\nNo conclusion is asserted when a measurable posterior minimizer does not\nexist. All risks may be $+\\infty$."
    ],
    "ex-squared-error-risk-for-bernoulli-observation": [],
